Rep. Antonio L. Tinio: meant to penalize countries that import from China, among others. Diba ganun? O, huwag na tayo maglokohan. Yun yun eh. Sige, please.

6068
05:34:05,638 --> 05:34:23,120
DTI Undersecretary Allan B. Gepty: Thank you, ma'am, and good afternoon, Your Honors. Number one, with respect to the imposition of tariffs by the United States, I think in terms of context, Po, Your Honor, we have to understand that when President Trump,

6069
05:34:23,120 --> 05:34:47,160
DTI Undersecretary Allan B. Gepty: assumed office, the policy of the United States has been to impose unilateral tariffs. So, basically, the direction is really for them to hit countries wherein they have deficit. But, of course, the first unilateral tariffs was declared unconstitutional by the US Supreme Court, and so they resorted again to another basis by which they can impose unilateral tariffs.

6070
05:34:47,558 --> 05:34:53,418
DTI Undersecretary Allan B. Gepty: After that, they imposed Section 122 tariffs, which can only last for 150 days.

6071
05:34:53,878 --> 05:35:13,980
DTI Undersecretary Allan B. Gepty: And then, after the expiration of Section 1 to 2, now they initiated the investigation under Section 301, and that's the basis by which they have imposed the 12.5% tariffs for, I think, 67 countries, including the Philippines, and for, I think, only around 10 countries, 10%.

6072
05:35:15,200 --> 05:35:22,780
DTI Undersecretary Allan B. Gepty: Now, the basis of Section 301 tariffs under the Trade Regulation Act of the United States, Your Honor, is actually...

6073
05:35:23,558 --> 05:35:38,760
DTI Undersecretary Allan B. Gepty: premise on the fact that countries or economies trading with the United States has no law that bans or prohibits the importation of goods which are made or results of forced labor.

6074
05:35:39,600 --> 05:35:52,058
DTI Undersecretary Allan B. Gepty: So, that's the basis. So, in other words, it doesn't mean that a particular country or economy has a weak regime when it comes to combating, for example, forced labor.

6075
05:35:52,878 --> 05:36:07,460
DTI Undersecretary Allan B. Gepty: So, in the case of the Philippines, we issued a joint administrative order institutionalizing the mechanism by which we can address that particular concern of the United States.

6076
05:36:07,958 --> 05:36:17,558
DTI Undersecretary Allan B. Gepty: And the same is under evaluation now of the US for purposes of at least compliance to their Section 301 investigation.

6077
05:36:18,120 --> 05:36:19,740
DTI Undersecretary Allan B. Gepty: So, iyon po ngayon iyong status ngayon.

6078
05:36:20,680 --> 05:36:22,440
DTI Undersecretary Allan B. Gepty: Negotiations are still ongoing.

6079
05:36:23,120 --> 05:36:26,600
DTI Undersecretary Allan B. Gepty: In fact, itong recent issuance po ng JAO, under evaluation.

6080
05:36:26,600 --> 05:36:38,958
DTI Undersecretary Allan B. Gepty: But we have highlighted the fact that the Philippines has a strong legal regime as far as addressing forced labor from the Constitution, laws, rules and regulations, among others po.

6081
05:36:38,958 --> 05:36:41,180
DTI Undersecretary Allan B. Gepty: So, yan po yung naging basihan po nun.

